“Seeing a horse galloping up on the road,the emperor's concubine smiled happily;no one but her knew the Lichee is coming.”
The method of transporting fresh litchi in the Han and Tang Dynasties is as recorded in Historical Annals of Litchi in the Han and Tang Dynasties on “Fresh Lichee Delivery”, together with branches and leaves,a ball of litchi wrapped in wet bamboo paper was put into a big diameter (more than10 cm) bamboo and then sealed with wax. After swift horse running day and night with non-stop from south to the North-west, the Lichee is still so fresh.
The then problem was that the fish was from the Yangtze River, thousands of miles away from Beijing, and in addition,hilsa herring was so delicate and easy to die. How could emperors eat fresh shad in Beijing?
According to historical records, "thick pig lard plus ice make a good storage".In advance, they boiled a large barrel of lard oil, then when it cooled down before solidification, just caught the fresh shad into oil barrel. When lard oil got solidified, it prevented the fish from the outside word,equivalent to the vacuum packaging, so that the fish was still fresh as they arrived in Beijing by swift riding,day and night.
Legend has it that Emperor Yongzheng also loved litchi. In order to curry favor with the emperor, Man Bao, then governor of Fujian and Zhejiang, often sent local specialties to Yongzheng.
It was a brilliant idea. Instead of just giving lychees, he sent a tree planted in a barrel that had already produced lychees.
